1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:04,140
Good morning everyone! Today I want
to give you a brief history of artificial

2
00:00:04,289 --> 00:00:08,400
intelligence research, from its formal
beginnings in the 1950s right up to the

3
00:00:08,401 --> 00:00:11,041
present day. Let's start
right at the beginning.

4
00:00:11,065 --> 00:00:12,972
The term "artificial
intelligence"

5
00:00:13,110 --> 00:00:18,240
was first coined in 1956, giving a name
to a field that researchers had only just

6
00:00:18,241 --> 00:00:23,640
begun to define. That name emerged at a
specific, now famous, event. The conference

7
00:00:23,641 --> 00:00:27,080
where the term was first used was the
Dartmouth Conference, held at Dartmouth

8
00:00:27,105 --> 00:00:30,680
College in the United States, where a
small group of researchers gathered to

9
00:00:30,681 --> 00:00:34,280
discuss the possibility of machine
intelligence. In those early years,

10
00:00:34,281 --> 00:00:39,620
progress was actually quite promising.
Early AI programs in the 1950s and 60s

11
00:00:39,769 --> 00:00:44,000
focused on logic problems, using symbolic
reasoning to solve puzzles and prove

12
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:49,480
mathematical theorems. That early optimism
didn't last, however. The 1970s saw a

13
00:00:49,629 --> 00:00:54,480
period of funding cuts, often referred to
now as the AI winter, when governments and

14
00:00:54,481 --> 00:00:58,480
investors grew disillusioned with the
field's slow progress. Interest revived

15
00:00:58,481 --> 00:01:02,960
somewhat in the following decade. A type
of program popular in the 1980s was the

16
00:01:03,010 --> 00:01:07,120
expert system, designed to capture the
specialized knowledge of human experts in a

17
00:01:07,121 --> 00:01:11,920
particular field, like medical diagnosis.
These systems relied heavily on rule-based

18
00:01:11,921 --> 00:01:16,480
logic, essentially long lists of if-then
rules written by hand by knowledge

19
00:01:16,481 --> 00:01:18,688
engineers working
with human experts.

20
00:01:18,712 --> 00:01:21,592
Unfortunately, expert
systems also fell short of

21
00:01:21,729 --> 00:01:25,580
expectations eventually, and what
caused a second decline in AI funding was

22
00:01:25,580 --> 00:01:29,260
largely unmet expectations, as
these systems proved brittle and

23
00:01:29,261 --> 00:01:33,420
expensive to maintain in the real world.
The field's fortunes changed again with a

24
00:01:33,421 --> 00:01:37,740
shift in approach. The technique that
became dominant from the 1990s onward was

25
00:01:37,741 --> 00:01:41,820
machine learning, where systems learn
patterns from data rather than following

26
00:01:41,821 --> 00:01:46,540
rules written explicitly by programmers.
One dramatic public demonstration of AI's

27
00:01:46,639 --> 00:01:52,380
growing capability came in 1997, when
IBM's Deep Blue defeated a chess champion,

28
00:01:52,529 --> 00:01:55,494
beating the reigning world
champion Garry Kasparov in

29
00:01:55,518 --> 00:01:58,547
a widely publicized match.
As we moved into the 2000s,

30
00:01:58,548 --> 00:02:01,184
another factor became
crucial to progress.

31
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:05,032
Big data, that is enormous
quantities of digital information,

32
00:02:05,144 --> 00:02:07,732
became crucial for modern
AI, giving machine learning

33
00:02:07,756 --> 00:02:10,551
systems far more material
to learn from than ever before.

34
00:02:10,552 --> 00:02:13,329
Alongside data,
hardware mattered too.

35
00:02:13,353 --> 00:02:17,752
GPUs, originally designed for
rendering video game graphics,

36
00:02:17,890 --> 00:02:20,163
turned out to be
extremely well suited to the

37
00:02:20,187 --> 00:02:22,472
mathematical operations
behind deep learning,

38
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,199
and this hardware advance
accelerated deep learning enormously.

39
00:02:26,223 --> 00:02:28,392
A pivotal moment
came in 2012, with a

40
00:02:28,416 --> 00:02:34,644
competition called ImageNet, in which a deep learning
system dramatically outperformed previous approaches

41
00:02:34,668 --> 00:02:39,035
at recognizing images, convincing many skeptical
researchers that deep learning was the way forward.

42
00:02:39,505 --> 00:02:45,963
Public excitement grew further in 2016, when the AI
system AlphaGo famously won at the ancient board game

43
00:02:45,987 --> 00:02:51,736
Go, defeating a top human professional in a game long
considered far too complex for computers to master.

44
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:54,107
Much of the progress in
image recognition around

45
00:02:54,131 --> 00:02:56,392
this time relied on a
particular architecture.

46
00:02:56,530 --> 00:02:59,019
The convolutional neural
network became the standard

47
00:02:59,043 --> 00:03:01,352
type of neural network
commonly used for images,

48
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:05,580
since its structure is particularly well
suited to recognizing visual patterns.

49
00:03:05,630 --> 00:03:10,140
More recently, a different architecture
has transformed another area entirely.

50
00:03:10,141 --> 00:03:13,632
Natural language processing saw
major progress with transformer

51
00:03:13,656 --> 00:03:17,272
models, which now underpin most
modern language-based AI systems,

52
00:03:17,310 --> 00:03:20,140
including translation
and text generation tools.

53
00:03:20,190 --> 00:03:22,573
Now, alongside all
this technical progress,

54
00:03:22,597 --> 00:03:25,272
there are important
concerns we need to discuss.

55
00:03:25,373 --> 00:03:30,183
One ethical concern the lecturer highlights
is algorithmic bias, where AI systems can

56
00:03:30,227 --> 00:03:35,032
unintentionally reproduce or even amplify
unfair patterns present in their training data.

57
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:38,300
There's also a growing practical
concern about sustainability.

58
00:03:38,350 --> 00:03:42,229
A resource-heavy issue about large
models is their energy consumption, since

59
00:03:42,253 --> 00:03:45,992
training the largest systems can
require enormous amounts of electricity.

60
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:50,060
Despite all this progress, some
challenges remain stubbornly difficult.

61
00:03:50,210 --> 00:03:53,450
What's still difficult for AI systems
is common sense reasoning,

62
00:03:53,474 --> 00:03:56,472
the kind of everyday understanding
humans take for granted,

63
00:03:56,610 --> 00:03:59,500
but which machines still
struggle to replicate reliably.

64
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:03,420
Looking ahead, researchers often
discuss a longer-term goal for the field.

65
00:04:03,570 --> 00:04:07,392
The term describing AI matching
general human ability is general

66
00:04:07,416 --> 00:04:11,192
intelligence, sometimes called
artificial general intelligence,

67
00:04:11,330 --> 00:04:14,540
a capability no current
system has fully achieved.

68
00:04:14,690 --> 00:04:17,980
To finish today, I'd like you to
prepare for next week's seminar.

69
